ok - a task whose own copy landed closes beside a foreign copy with unique ignored files, which stays byte-identical, with the slot still reserved
ok - a task whose own copy landed closes beside a foreign copy with commits on no remote, which stays byte-identical, with the slot still reserved
ok - each held foreign copy gets one record line with UTC time, closing task, slot, copy and its exact reason
ok - a held close removes the task's own nested copy, through the driver when it is locked, and leaves the slot, its lease and claim and the held foreign copy alone
ok - a foreign copy inside the own nested copy is never held or swept away: the close refuses and every copy, the slot, its lease and claim stay as they were
ok - an ordinary close whose slot gains a copy holding unique files during the project-lock wait refuses with nothing removed
ok - a stale-lock cleanup refuses when the slot gained a copy holding unique files, instead of turning the close into a held close
ok - a held close reserves a plain-get slot under a durable lease before it removes anything and leaves the held copy byte-identical
ok - a held close refuses before it removes anything when the slot cannot be durably leased, and says why
ok - a held close lists every held copy, says the slot is reserved under a durable lease and stays claimed, and promises no cleanup that does not exist
ok - a held close refused by a later step writes no record line, and the close that completes writes one
ok - a held-copy record that cannot be written prints a warning on stdout and the close still completes with the foreign copy untouched
ok - a --legacy-record close beside a held foreign copy still lists the held copy and says the slot return is deferred
ok - a dirty or unlanded own copy still refuses exactly as before when a foreign copy would be held, and nothing changes
ok - a foreign copy that is also dirty, named by another record, or running a process still refuses the whole close
ok - a copy not held because a process of the closing task runs in it, or launched its lock, is refused naming that process, and nothing is signalled or removed
ok - a foreign copy whose landing could not be checked is refused with nothing removed and no record line, and a rerun releases a landed copy and holds an unlanded one
ok - a failure after the task record and own copies are gone still leaves the held notice and one record line, and the foreign copy untouched
ok - --force waives nothing: it never holds a foreign copy and still refuses
ok - a slot with no foreign copy closes and returns exactly as before and writes no held-copy record
ok - uncommitted files make a foreign copy refuse the close
